    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Confucius had a major impact on ancient China’s view of justice and law. Confucius and his disciples believed a harmonious society depended on five relationships: ruler and subject, husband and wife, father and son, older and younger brother, and friend and friend. They believed that when everyone worked on being a good person, cared for these relationships, and fulfilled their responsibilities, society wouldn’t need strict laws or punishments.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "Ancient China + How have philosophers defined justice?",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "The imperial Chinese code during the Qin dynasty (221-206 BCE) rejected Confucius’ teachings in favor of Legalism, which taught that humans were naturally violent. Legalism taught that to prevent humans from falling into their natural, evil state, the government needed to take full control.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "Ancient China + How have philosophers defined justice?",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Li Si, the counselor to the first emperor, created a harsh code following the unification of China. The emperor appointed district officials who served as judges and investigators. Those accused of crimes were guilty until proven innocent, and trials didn’t have lawyers or juries. Punishments varied based on the crime.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "Ancient China + How have philosophers defined justice?",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Punishments were harsh; minor violations could lead to fines, hard labor, beatings, and banishment, while serious crimes could result in mutilations, castration, and death.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "Ancient China + How have philosophers defined justice?",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "When the Han Dynasty (202 BCE-220 CE) made Confucianism the state philosophy, China’s justice system softened.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0007",
        "chunk_title": "Ancient China + How have philosophers defined justice?",
        "chunk_order": 6,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
